# Lesson 3, new example hand: take your time at trick 1. Throw a diamond loser away before you let the defense in with the ♠A.
title: First things first
id: L03-14
type: example
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.1, 3.3
student: S
board: 46
dealer: E
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  876     AKJ4   6542   53
  E  43      Q8762  9      QJT42
  S  KQJT92  5      A873   AK
  W  A5      T93    KQJT   9876

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1S     = 13+ points, at least four spades
  W Pass
  N 2S     = 6-9 points and spade support
  E Pass
  S 4S     = Game
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ with 17 high-card points and a six-card suit, and partner
  raised. That was enough for game. You are declarer, because you bid spades
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 1 = The ace.
  hearts: 0 = None, and dummy's ♥K is an extra winner.
  diamonds: 3 = Three small ones. One may go away on dummy's ♥K, but only before the defense gets in.
  clubs: 0 = Solid.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand.
  remind: What will the defense do when they win the ♠A?
play:
  1   W DK  N D2  E D9  S DA
      note: Declarer stops to count. Four losers: the ♠A and three diamonds. If declarer draws trumps now, West wins the ace and takes three diamonds: down one. But dummy's ♥K is an extra winner, and declarer has no hearts after the first round. So first get rid of a diamond.
      pause
  2   S H5  W H3  N HA  E H2
      note: Over to dummy's ♥A...
  3   N HK  E H6  S D3  W H9
      note: ...and throw a diamond away on the ♥K. Now declarer has only three losers.
  4   N S6  E S3  S SK  W SA
      note: Now it's safe to start on trumps. West wins the ace.
  5   W DQ  N D4  E C2  S D7
      note: West takes the diamond tricks...
  6   W DJ  N D5  E C4  S D8
  7   W DT  N D6  E H7  S S2
      note: ...but declarer is out of diamonds now, and ruffs the third one.
  8   S SQ  W S5  N S7  E S4
      note: Draw the last trumps.
  9   S CA  W C6  N C3  E CT
      note: The rest are winners.
  10  S CK  W C7  N C5  E CJ
      note: The ♣K.
  11  S SJ  W C8  N S8  E CQ
      note: The rest are all trumps.
  12  S ST  W C9  N H4  E H8
      note: Another trump.
  13  S S9  W HT  N HJ  E HQ
      note: 10 tricks: 4♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: count four losers, throw one away on dummy's ♥K, and only then
  draw trumps.

  Usually you draw trumps first. But here the opponents held the ♠A, so
  drawing trumps meant giving them the lead, and they had diamond tricks
  waiting. Declarer counted at trick 1, saw the danger, and got rid of a
  loser first.
